<h3>Understanding Huntington&#x27;s Disease and Its Progressive Neurological Effects</h3>
<p>Huntington&#x27;s Disease (HD) is a rare, inherited neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the progressive breakdown of nerve cells in the brain. Over time, HD damages specialized brain structures—specifically the basal ganglia and cerebral cortex—leading to involuntary movements, cognitive decline, and psychiatric disturbances.</p>
<p>Because Huntington&#x27;s Disease affects multiple neurological systems simultaneously, managing its progression requires complex, multidisciplinary medical care. As symptom severity advances, families often look toward global research hubs to understand emerging therapeutic concepts.</p>
<h3>Why Malaysian Families Seek Information About Emerging Neurological Research</h3>
