<h3>How Huntington&#x27;s Disease Affects Movement, Cognition, and Daily Independence</h3>
<h3>Motor Dysfunction</h3>
<p>Involuntary jerking or writhing movements (chorea), rigidity, impaired gait, difficulty swallowing (dysphagia), and loss of physical balance.</p>
<h3>Cognitive Impairment</h3>
<p>Loss of executive function, difficulty organizing tasks, reduced processing speed, memory loss, and impaired spatial awareness.</p>
<h3>Loss of Independence</h3>
<p>Gradual inability to manage daily personal care, employment, communication, mobility, and self-care without full-time assistance.</p>
<h3>Understanding the Genetic Cause of Huntington&#x27;s Disease</h3>
<p>Huntington&#x27;s Disease is caused by a mutation in the HTT gene, located on chromosome 4. This gene encodes a protein called huntingtin. The mutation involves an abnormal expansion of a CAG (cytosine-adenine-guanine) trinucleotide repeat sequence:</p>
<p>Normal Repeat Count: 10 to 35 CAG repeats.</p>
<p>Intermediate Count: 36 to 39 CAG repeats (reduced penetrance; may or may not develop symptoms).</p>
<p>Pathological Count: 40 or more CAG repeats (full penetrance; individual will develop HD).</p>
<p>This expanded CAG segment causes the production of an abnormally long, toxic mutant huntingtin protein (mHTT). Over time, mHTT misfolds, accumulates inside striatal neurons, disrupts intracellular transport, and ultimately triggers premature cell death.</p>
<h3>What Current Medical Science Knows About Huntington&#x27;s Disease Progression</h3>
<p>Epidemiological and epidemiological data published by official health organizations show that Huntington&#x27;s Disease typically manifests between ages 30 and 50, though juvenile onset can occur. Once clinical motor signs emerge, the disease progresses continuously over an average duration of 15 to 20 years.</p>
